cell64lv2: make wchar_t 16-bit and wint_t int under ILP32, as in the reference.

The reference toolchain's wchar_t is a 16-bit unsigned short and its
wint_t is int.  Ours was a 32-bit long int and unsigned int.  This is a
layout difference, not only a naming one: sizeof(wchar_t) was 4 instead
of 2, L"" literals were UTF-32 instead of UTF-16, structs holding wchar_t
had a different size and offsets, and code passing L"" to interfaces
that take UTF-16 misread the text.

Under ILP32, set WCHAR_TYPE to "short unsigned int", WCHAR_TYPE_SIZE to
16 and WINT_TYPE to "int".  newlib's wide-character code supports a
16-bit wchar_t, so it needs a rebuild, not a patch; so does libstdc++.
(This newlib is built without multibyte support, so its conversions map
one byte to one wchar_t either way.)  The LP64 data model has no
reference counterpart and keeps a 32-bit wchar_t.

This changes the size of wchar_t: C and C++ objects built with an earlier
PS3DK that use wchar_t must be rebuilt.

--- a/gcc/config/rs6000/cell64lv2.h
+++ b/gcc/config/rs6000/cell64lv2.h
@@ -362,10 +362,18 @@
 #undef	PTRDIFF_TYPE
 #define	PTRDIFF_TYPE (TARGET_LP64 ? "long int" : "int")
 
+/* wchar_t and wint_t under ILP32 match the reference toolchain: wchar_t
+   is a 16-bit unsigned short (UTF-16 code units, so L"" literals and
+   structs holding wchar_t have the reference layout) and wint_t is int.
+   newlib's wide-character code supports a 16-bit wchar_t (this newlib
+   has no multibyte support).  The LP64 data model has no reference
+   counterpart and keeps a 32-bit wchar_t.  */
 #undef	WCHAR_TYPE
-#define	WCHAR_TYPE (TARGET_LP64 ? "int" : "long int")
+#define	WCHAR_TYPE (TARGET_LP64 ? "int" : "short unsigned int")
 #undef  WCHAR_TYPE_SIZE
-#define WCHAR_TYPE_SIZE 32
+#define WCHAR_TYPE_SIZE (TARGET_LP64 ? 32 : 16)
+#undef	WINT_TYPE
+#define	WINT_TYPE (TARGET_LP64 ? "unsigned int" : "int")
 
 /* The 64-bit ISA always defines TARGET_64BIT, so the rs6000.h
    (TARGET_32BIT ? 32 : 64) gate would lock long at 64. The data
